  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › Basic Japanese words</p>
  <h1>Basic Japanese words for beginners</h1>
  <p class="lede">These 15 basic Japanese words are the ones you will hear and use every day. Learn them with their romaji (Japanese written in English letters) and their meaning — then try them in a short sentence, because words you use are words you remember.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>15 basic Japanese words</h2><table class="word-table">
      <tr><th>Japanese</th><th>Romaji</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      <tr><td>こんにちは</td><td>konnichiwa</td><td>Hello / good afternoon</td></tr>
      <tr><td>おはようございます</td><td>ohayou gozaimasu</td><td>Good morning (polite)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>こんばんは</td><td>konbanwa</td><td>Good evening</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ありがとう</td><td>arigatou</td><td>Thank you</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ありがとうございます</td><td>arigatou gozaimasu</td><td>Thank you very much (polite)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>すみません</td><td>sumimasen</td><td>Excuse me / sorry</td></tr>
      <tr><td>はい</td><td>hai</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>いいえ</td><td>iie</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>お願いします</td><td>onegai shimasu</td><td>Please (when asking for something)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>どうぞ</td><td>douzo</td><td>Here you go / please, go ahead</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ごめんなさい</td><td>gomen nasai</td><td>Sorry (for a mistake)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>さようなら</td><td>sayounara</td><td>Goodbye</td></tr>
      <tr><td>おいしい</td><td>oishii</td><td>Delicious</td></tr>
      <tr><td>大丈夫</td><td>daijoubu</td><td>It is OK / I am fine</td></tr>
      <tr><td>わかります</td><td>wakarimasu</td><td>I understand</td></tr>
    </table>
  <h2>How to use these words</h2>
  <p>Two of these words do more work than any others: すみません (sumimasen) and ありがとう (arigatou). Sumimasen means “excuse me”, “sorry” and “thank you” all at once, depending on the situation — you will hear it everywhere in Japan. Arigatou is the everyday “thank you”; add ございます (gozaimasu) to make it polite.</p>
  <p>はい (hai) means “yes”, but in conversation it often just means “I am listening” — Japanese speakers say hai while listening, the way English speakers say “mm-hm”. And どうぞ (douzo) is the word you use when offering something: “douzo” as you hand someone a cup of tea.</p>
  <h2>From words to sentences</h2>
  <p>Once you know these words, combine them into your first sentences. “Konnichiwa, watashi wa [your name] desu” means “Hello, I am [your name]”. “Sumimasen, wakarimasen” means “Sorry, I don’t understand”. “Oishii desu!” means “It’s delicious!” — three words you will use constantly in Japan.</p>
  <p>The next step is learning hiragana, so you can read these words in real Japanese instead of romaji. Romaji is fine for your first words, but every Japanese book, sign and menu uses hiragana, katakana and kanji.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Only learning romaji and never learning hiragana — real Japanese does not use romaji.</li>
    <li>Using さようなら (sayounara) for every goodbye — it sounds final; friends usually say じゃあね (jaa ne) or またね (mata ne).</li>
    <li>Saying ありがとう to everyone — use ありがとうございます with strangers and older people.</li>
    <li>Learning words alone, without ever putting them in a sentence.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn 5 words a day, and use each one in a sentence the same day.</li>
    <li>Practise saying the words aloud — Japanese pronunciation is very regular.</li>
    <li>Listen for these words in anime, dramas or songs; hearing them in real speech makes them stick.</li>
    <li>Learn hiragana next, so you can read these words in real Japanese.</li>
  </ul>

