  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How many Japanese words do I need to start speaking?</summary><p>You can start with just these 15 words plus a few verbs like “to be”, “to eat” and “to go”. With 100 words you can handle greetings, ordering food and asking simple questions. With 500–1,000 words you can manage most everyday situations.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is romaji enough to learn Japanese?</summary><p>For your very first words, yes. But romaji hides the real sounds and no Japanese material uses it, so learn hiragana as soon as you can — most learners manage it in about two weeks of short daily practice.</p></details>
  <details><summary>What is the difference between sumimasen and gomen nasai?</summary><p>Sumimasen is the everyday “excuse me” or light “sorry” — getting attention, passing someone, or a small thank-you. Gomen nasai is a stronger apology for a real mistake. In daily life you will use sumimasen far more often.</p></details>
  <details><summary>How do I say “please” in Japanese?</summary><p>It depends on the situation. お願いします (onegai shimasu) is “please” when you are asking for something. どうぞ (douzo) is “please” when you are offering something or inviting someone to go ahead.</p></details>
